WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:09.910
For the past 25 years, there has been a tremendous
progress in cancer research.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:15.500
For example, we know that only 5-10% of all
cancer cases can be attributed to genetic

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:22.230
defects, whereas the remaining 90 - 95% have
their roots in the environment and lifestyle.&nbsp;

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:27.220
We also know that all cancer is the result
of gene mutations.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.689
And these mutations are the fundamental cause
that transform a normal cell into cancerous

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:36.600
cells, that could sustain uncontrolled cell
growth, avoid immune destruction, activating

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.829
metastasis, and etc, commonly known as the
10 hallmarks of cancer.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:45.850
Importantly, there have been tremendous effort
and investment in developing new treatments

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.890
targeting these hallmarks with the hope to
kill the tumor cells in our body without much

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.960
harm to the normal cells.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.770
As a result, the cancer survival outcome has
improved significantly from 1 in 4 in the

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:03.129
1970s, to today’s survival rate of more
than 50%, and hopefully in the future, we

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:07.230
can increase the survival rate to 3 in 4 by
2034.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:11.950
Remarkably, treatment for some cancers have
performed much better compared to others,

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.950
for example, prostate cancer, breast cancer
and Hodgkin Lymphoma has achieved more than

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:22.640
90% survival rate while other hard to treat
cancers such as brain cancer, glioblastoma,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.700
and pancreatic cancers, the survival rate
remained unchanged in the past 25 years and

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:33.320
management of these diseases remained a challenge
due to lack of treatment option.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:38.380
Thanks to the advancement of the sequencing
technology, such as the Next generation Sequencing

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.740
or NGS technology, the cost of sequencing
of a human genome has reduced from USD 100

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.020
million in year 2000 when the first human
genome sequence project was launched, to close

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.550
to about USD 1000.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:59.060
In fact, back in 2019, the Beijing Genomics
Institute offered a service for whole genome

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.940
sequencing with the cost of USD 600 per sample.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.450
This is even cheaper than your iPhone!

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:11.500
Today, we have more than 50 petabytes of open
dataset related to cancers, equivalent to

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.459
playing mp3 in your iPhone for 100 thousand
years.

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:21.610
The question then became, How to harness the
power of these data for better cure of cancers?

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.709
The cancer problem suddenly became a big data
problem.

00:02:26.709 --> 00:02:31.050
One way how the big data from cancer research
can be used is to improve the precision of

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.319
cancer treatment based on the biology of the
tumor.

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:41.430
For example, back in year 2003, by analyzing
the gene expression of close to about 100

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:46.930
primary tumors from breast cancer patients,
scientists were able to identify 5 major subtype

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.939
of breast cancers using unsupervised hierarchical
clustering.

00:02:50.939 --> 00:02:56.090
Particularly, 2 of the subtypes that have
demonstrate a clinical significance was the

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:00.879
luminal-like and the basal-like subtype, or
sometime known as the triple negative breast

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:05.230
cancers, where the basal-like subtype breast
cancer has significantly worst prognosis.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:11.989
Being ER, PR and HER2 negative, the basal-like
tumors do not response to the hormonal therapy

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:14.230
or Herceptin treatment.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:18.269
The only option at the time was conventional
chemotherapy.

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:23.609
Even that, the response rate was often very
low, in less than 15%.

00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:27.620
These findings have grab the attention of
the breast cancer research at that time and

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:33.069
a lot of research was since conducted to target
this refractory subtype of breast cancers.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:37.980
Today, there are many options for treatments,
including carboplatinum and PARP inhibitors

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.989
to target the BRCA-ness of the tumors, and
PD-L1 immune checkpoint inhibitors such as

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.779
Atezolizumab.

00:03:44.779 --> 00:03:49.959
Of note, this was done 20 years ago before
the explosion of the cancer data and the availability

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:51.709
of the advanced algorithms.

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:59.019
In 2006, a massive effort to catalog the genomics
and biological changes in cancer cells was

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:00.019
launched.

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:06.819
The Cancer Genome Atlas project, or TCGA in
short, is a 10-year project that aims to characterize

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:12.489
the molecular landscape of tumors from more
than 11,000 patients across 33 cancer types,

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:15.580
and defined their many molecular subtypes.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:22.140
At the completion of the project in 2016,
a total of 2.5 petabytes of data was curated,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:27.690
across multi-omics platforms, such as genomics,
epigenomics and proteomics, together with

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.260
comprehensive clinical data.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.600
The beauty of the project was that, all data
was made accessible by the public under the

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.290
open science platform, and anyone or company
are welcomed to use those data to improve

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:44.500
cancer care with no restriction on IP rights
whatsoever.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:49.660
Using the dataset, the first question we asked
was, are all the cancers similar or how similar

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:54.680
are they from one type of cancer versus another
type of cancer, biologically?

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.330
It may sound like a naïve question.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.380
However, if you think about it, most of the
time we define the cancers based on their

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.430
anatomical site.

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:08.050
For example, if you have a cancer in the breast,
we called it breast cancer; if you have a

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.190
cancer in the lung, we called it lung cancer
and so forth.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:16.740
The fundamental question is that, if cancer
arise from genetic mutations, will all the

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.790
cancers share some kind of similarity, or
will they be different biologically.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:27.390
The finding was, as anticipated, 90% of the
cancer, do have distinct biology based on

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.480
their cell of origin.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.120
This is reassuring as the diagnosis and treatment
that we practice today is highly depend on

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.280
the cell or origin classification.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:42.190
However, what was quite interesting was that,
10% of the tumor shown somewhat shared biology

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.860
with different type of cancers.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:49.940
For example, a subset of bladder cancer are
more similar to lung cancer, while breast

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:54.540
cancer as we know it, can be separated into
luminal-like and basal-like subtype with distinct

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.360
biology.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.300
These findings prompt us to think, whether
the drugs that we developed for one type of

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.200
cancer, say lung cancer, will be useful to
treat the subtype of bladder cancers that

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.190
has high similarity to lung cancer.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.810
If we can do that, it will help the patients
dramatically, as bladder cancer is considered

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.720
as a type of rare cancer with limited treatment,
while treatment for lung cancers are widely

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:18.980
available.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:23.190
With the profiling data, we have also established
a clear picture on the difference between

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.700
normal and tumor cells.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.780
Of course, in biology, we understand that
correlation doesn’t mean causation.

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:35.700
The biological differences between tumor and
normal tissue can be the cause of the disease,

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.510
or it could be the consequences of the disease.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:44.870
To understand the causative role of the different
genes in cancer, the project DRIVE, an acronym

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:50.470
for Deep RNAi interrogation of viability effects
in cancer, was launched to identify what genes

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:55.240
are required for the survival of different
type of tumor cells.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:00.750
By combining the dataset from the TCGA project
which identify genes that are commonly up-regulated

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:05.170
in cancers, and the dataset from the DRIVE
project which identified the cancer driver

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:10.210
genes that is needed for cancer cells to survive,
we can identify targets which can potentially

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:14.990
be useful for future development of targeted
therapy.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.880
Another way how the cancer genomic data can
be used, is to mine the data for drug repurposing

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:25.310
in order to reduce the cost and time of drug
discovery and development.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:29.810
In average, it is estimated that the Cost
to Develop New Pharmaceutical Drug is bout

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:35.930
$2.5B and take about 10 years from discovery
to clinic.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:40.300
It takes hundred of thousand of compounds
to be screened in the lab, to get 1 drug that

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.820
will eventually be approved by FDA.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:46.130
That’s if you’re lucky!

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:50.120
This is further complicated with the shortening
of the life-cycle of drugs in the marker due

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:55.810
to rapid appearing of the competitor drug
in the market with better efficacy and profile.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:00.840
Some were even needed to be withdraw from
the market before the patent ended.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:07.280
As a result, oncology drug process increase
more than 100-fold in the past 30 years.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.060
Because of these, not only the patients need
to bear the toxic side effects of the drugs,

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.130
the family too need to bear the financial
toxicity of cancer treatment.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:16.130
Indeed, in the recent report published, majority
of Malaysian will only be able to survive

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:17.130
one year when they are diagnose with cancer,
before they go bankrupt and died.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:18.130
Imagine, each targeted therapy cost about
RM 50k to RM 400k.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.130
How many people can afford it?

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.150
However, if we can repurpose some of the drugs
in the market, the cost and the time of development

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.370
can be dramatically reduced.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:30.530
The question is, how can we rapidly and efficiently
discover new indications for existing drugs

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:32.710
with known safety profiles?

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:36.699
Should we go by the traditional high-throughput
screen?

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:43.149
Or can we leverage on the existing data and
AI to identify these drugs candidates?

00:08:43.149 --> 00:08:47.399
For example, in pancreatic cancers.

00:08:47.399 --> 00:08:52.440
Pancreatic cancer is one of the deadliest
cancers with only 5% survival rate.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:58.430
Unfortunately, being a deadly and rare cancer,
not many treatment option are available.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:03.770
The only chemotherapy drug available for pancreatic
cancer is gemcitabine-based regime which has

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:06.810
been used for more than 40 years.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:11.810
Attempt to combined with targeted therapy
such as EGFR inhibitor, failed to demonstrate

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.040
benefit to patients.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:19.629
We need to rapidly identify drugs that could
treat this refractory cancers.

00:09:19.629 --> 00:09:24.379
By using the data derived from large scale
genomic studies, it was shown that pancreatic

00:09:24.379 --> 00:09:29.389
cancers are highly heterogenous and can be
stratified into 4 major subtype group based

00:09:29.389 --> 00:09:31.360
on their biology.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.389
The so-called squamous subtype of pancreatic
cancer has a significant worse clinical outcome

00:09:36.389 --> 00:09:40.970
compare to other subtype of pancreatic cancer.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:45.999
Based on the gene signature that was identified
from the squamous subtype of pancreatic cancer,

00:09:45.999 --> 00:09:50.050
we can compare the differentially expressed
genes in pancreatic cancers with the gene

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:54.769
that are induced by different drugs curated
in the Connectivity Map database, which consists

00:09:54.769 --> 00:10:00.949
of gene profile of more than 40,000 drugs,
and more than 1 billion data points.

00:10:00.949 --> 00:10:06.040
The idea was to find a drug that could potentially
revert the gene signature of the pancreatic

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.870
cancer.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:13.670
The results show that the SRC inhibitor, EGFR
inhibitors, and potentially IGF1 inhibitors

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:18.930
might be useful to treat the squamous subtype
of pancreatic cancer.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:23.620
We then prioritize the drug candidate that
are FDA-approved for further testing and showed

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:28.550
that, almost all the SRC inhibitor tested
show synergism with gemcitabine in the squamous-like

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:33.600
pancreatic cancer, as shown in the blue color
contour here, compare to other subtype of

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.050
the pancreatic cancer.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:40.800
Based on these results, a number of clinical
trials are currently ongoing to evaluate the

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.360
efficacy of SRC inhibitors for treatment of
pancreatic cancers.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:50.920
Finally, we can also use open data source
to develop computer vision algorthm, to quicken

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:57.029
the process of screening and reduce mis-diagnosis
of cancer which can be as high as 35%, especially

00:10:57.029 --> 00:10:59.560
when it comes to radiology.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:05.080
The idea was that, if we can combine the wisdom
of experience radiologist in different subfield,

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:09.360
we hope that the AI system trained by these
experts will be able to standardize and improve

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.319
the accuracy of detecting cancers earlier.

00:11:13.319 --> 00:11:18.029
For example, MIT’s Computer Science and
Artificial Intelligence Lab has developed

00:11:18.029 --> 00:11:22.970
a new deep learning-based AI prediction model
that can anticipate the development of breast

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.660
cancer up to five years in advance.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:30.559
The algorithm, which was trained on mammograms
and patient outcomes from over 60,000 patients

00:11:30.559 --> 00:11:35.260
from the Massachusetts General Hospital, uses
deep learning to identify patterns that would

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:39.529
not be apparent or even observable by human
clinicians.

00:11:39.529 --> 00:11:44.079
Because it’s not based on existing assumptions
or received knowledge about risk factors,

00:11:44.079 --> 00:11:48.519
which are at best a suggestive framework,
the results have so far shown to be far more

00:11:48.519 --> 00:11:52.649
accurate, especially at predictive, pre-diagnosis
discovery.

00:11:52.649 --> 00:11:57.810
Overall, the project is intended to help healthcare
professionals put together the right screening

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.339
program for individuals in their care and
eliminate the heartbreaking and all-too common

00:12:02.339 --> 00:12:04.949
outcome of late diagnosis.

00:12:04.949 --> 00:12:08.959
The technique can also be used to improve
detection of other diseases that have similar

00:12:08.959 --> 00:12:15.990
problems with existing risk models which has
far too many gaps and lower degrees of accuracy.

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:21.740
In summary, cancer genomics and big data that
makes use of advanced technologies, such as

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:27.529
DNA sequencing technology and digital pathology,
give scientists enormous power to uncover

00:12:27.529 --> 00:12:32.339
how these biological and genomic changes drive
cancer formation and growth.

00:12:32.339 --> 00:12:37.600
However, despite the wealth of data being
accumulated about cancer genomics, the actual

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.189
benefits are still murky.

00:12:40.189 --> 00:12:44.400
For people with a cancer mutation that can
be targeted by an available therapy, there

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.120
is no question that cancer-genome sequencing
leads to better outcomes and survival.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.440
But, if overall cancer-survival data are taken
as the endpoint, the benefits might not yet

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.279
be as clear.

00:12:56.279 --> 00:13:00.449
The number of known cancer-causing mutations
still far outweighs the number of treatments

00:13:00.449 --> 00:13:02.980
targeting those mutations.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:07.160
We need to look at the impact that it has
on those very small subgroups, then we would

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.040
consider that the impact of genomics is really
big.

